[al:新概念英语（四）]
[ar:MP3 同步字幕版（美音）]
[ti:Are There Strangers in Space?]
[by:更多学习内容，请到VeryCD.com搜索“露珠”]
[00:00.55]Lesson 43 | 第43课
[00:03.12]Are there strangers in space? | 太空中有陌生人吗？
[00:11.44]What does the 'uniquely rational way' for us to communicate with other intelligent beings in space depend on? | 我们与太空中其他智慧生命联系的“独一无二合理的方式”取决于什么？
[00:22.80]We must conclude from the work of those who have studied the origin of life, | 我们必须从那些研究生命起源的人的著作中得出结论：
[00:28.36]that given a planet only approximately like our own, life is almost certain to start. | 只要有一颗行星与我们的地球大致相似，生命的开始几乎是肯定的。
[00:36.13]Of all the planets in our solar system, we are now pretty certain the Earth is the only one on which life can survive. | 在太阳系的所有行星中，我们现在相当肯定，地球是唯一一颗生命能够存活的行星。
[00:46.40]Mars is too dry and poor in oxygen, Venus far too hot, and so is Mercury, | 火星太干燥、缺氧，金星太热，水星也是如此，
[00:54.04]and the outer planets have temperatures near absolute zero and hydrogen-dominated atmospheres. | 而外行星的温度接近绝对零度，大气层以氢为主。
[01:02.12]But other suns, start as the astronomers call them, are bound to have planets like our own, and as is the number of stars in the universe is so vast, | 但其他太阳，即天文学家所说的恒星，肯定会有像我们地球一样的行星，而且既然宇宙中恒星的数量如此庞大，
[01:14.38]this possibility becomes virtual certainty. | 这种可能性实际上就成了必然。
[01:18.33]There are one hundred thousand million starts in our own Milky Way alone, | 仅我们银河系就有一千亿颗恒星，
[01:24.73]and then there are three thousand million other milky ways or galaxies, in the universe. | 而宇宙中还有三十亿个其他银河系即星系。
[01:32.54]so the number of stars that we know exist is now estimated at about 300 million million million. | 所以我们所知道存在的恒星数目，现在估计约为3万亿亿颗。
[01:43.29]Although perhaps only 1 percent of the life that has started somewhere will develop into highly complex and intelligent patterns, | 虽然在某个地方已经诞生的生命之中，也许只有百分之一会发展成高度复杂而智慧的类型，
[01:52.47]so vast is the number of planets, that intelligent life is bound to be a natural part of the universe. | 但行星的数目如此浩瀚，智慧生命必然是宇宙的一个自然组成部分。
[02:00.95]If then we are so certain that other intelligent life exists in the universe, why have we had no visitors from outer space yet? | 那么，既然我们如此确信宇宙中存在着其他智慧生命，为什么至今还没有外层空间的来客呢？
[02:10.93]First of all, they may have come to this planet of ours thousands or millions of years ago, | 首先，他们可能几千年或几百万年前就来过我们这颗行星，
[02:18.52]and found our then prevailing primitive state completely uninteresting to their own advanced knowledge. | 发现我们当时普遍存在的原始状态，对他们先进的知识来说完全索然无味。
[02:26.42]Professor Ronald Bracewell, a leading American radio astronomer, | 罗纳德·布雷斯韦尔教授——一位杰出的美国射电天文学家——
[02:32.01]argued in Nature that such a superior civilization, on a visit to our own solar system, | 在《自然》杂志上论证说，这样一个高级文明在访问我们的太阳系时，
[02:40.13]may have left an automatic messenger behind to await the possible awakening of an advanced civilization. | 可能留下了一个自动信使，等待某个先进文明可能的觉醒。
[02:48.54]Such a messenger, receiving our radio and television signals, | 这样一个信使，在接收到我们的无线电和电视信号之后，
[02:53.48]might well re-transmit them back to its home-planet, | 很可能把它们重新发射回它的母星，
[02:57.84]although what impression any other civilization would thus get from us is best left unsaid. | 尽管任何其他文明由此对我们产生的印象如何，还是不说为好。
[03:06.46]But here we come up against the most difficult of all obstacles to contact with people on other planets | 但在这里我们遇到了与外行星人联系的一切障碍中最困难的一个
[03:14.05]-- the astronomical distances which separate us. | ——即隔开我们的天文距离。
[03:18.51]As a reasonable guess, they might, on an average, be 100 light years away. | 作为一个合理的猜测，他们平均可能在100光年之外。
[03:26.43](A light year is the distance which light travels at 186, 000 miles per second in one year, namely 6 million million miles.) | （一光年是光以每秒186000英里的速度在一年中走过的距离，即6万亿英里。）
[03:38.43]Radio waves also travel at the speed of light, | 无线电波也以光速传播，
[03:42.04]and assuming such an automatic messenger picked up our first broadcasts of the 1920's, | 假如这样一个自动信使收到了我们20世纪20年代的最早广播，
[03:48.75]the message to its home planet is barely halfway there. | 传送到它母星的信息才刚刚走完一半路程。
[03:53.48]Similarly, our own present primitive chemical rockets, | 同样，我们现在原始的化学火箭，
[03:58.30]though good enough to orbit men, have no chance of transporting us to the nearest other star, | 虽然足以把人送入轨道，却毫无希望把我们送到最近的别的恒星，
[04:06.05]four light years away, let alone distances of tens or hundreds of light years. | 那颗星在4光年之外，更不用说几十或几百光年的距离了。
[04:13.85]Fortunately, there is a 'uniquely rational way' for us to communicate with other intelligent beings, | 幸而，存在着一种“独一无二合理的方式”使我们能与其他智慧生命进行联系，
[04:22.33]as Walter Sullivan has put it in his excellent book, | 正如沃尔特·沙利文在其杰作中所说的那样，
[04:26.34]We Are not Alone. | 书名是《我们不孤独》。
[04:28.60]This depends on the precise radio frequency of the 21-cm wavelength, or 1420 megacycles per second. | 这取决于21厘米波长、即每秒1420兆周的精确无线电频率。
[04:41.42]It is the natural frequency of emission of the hydrogen atoms in space and was discovered by us in 1951; | 这是太空中氢原子的自然辐射频率，是我们在1951年发现的；
[04:50.31]it must be known to any kind of radio astronomer in the universe. | 宇宙中任何一种射电天文学家都必定知道它。
[04:55.85]Once the existence of this wave-length had been discovered, | 一旦这种波长的存在被发现，
[04:59.84]it was not long before its use as the uniquely recognizable broadcasting frequency for interstellar communication was suggested. | 人们不久就提出把它用作星际通讯的独一无二的、可识别的广播频率。
[05:09.30]Without something of this kind, | 没有这类东西，
[05:11.49]searching for intelligences on other planets would be like trying to meet a friend in London without a pre-arranged rendezvous | 在其他行星上寻找智慧生命，就像想在没有预先约好会面地点的情况下在伦敦见到一位朋友，
[05:19.41]and absurdly wandering the streets in the hope of a chance encounter. | 荒唐地在街头游荡，指望碰巧相遇。
